# Business Case — GoalTrack

## 1. Problem / Opportunity
People often manage their goals and daily tasks in separate places, making it difficult to see how everyday tasks contribute to larger goals. A simple system that connects goals with tasks could help users stay organized and track progress more easily.

## 2. Proposed Solution
GoalTrack is a web application that allows users to create larger goals and break them into smaller, manageable tasks. Users can view their goals and tasks, select individual items to see details, mark tasks as complete, set due dates, and track progress toward their goals. The application provides one organized place to manage responsibilities and see how individual tasks contribute to larger goals.

## 3. Options Considered

| Option | Description | Pros | Cons |
|--------|-------------|------|------|
| Option A | Continue using separate tools such as notes, calendars, or basic to-do lists. | No development cost; familiar tools; easy to start. | Goals and tasks can remain disconnected; progress may be harder to track; information can be spread across multiple places. |
| Option B (recommended) | Create GoalTrack, a web application that connects goals with individual tasks and provides progress tracking. | Combines goals and tasks in one place; makes progress easier to understand; provides a focused user experience; can be expanded later. | Requires development time; users would need to learn and use another application. |

## 4. Feasibility

| Type | Assessment |
|------|------------|
| Operational — will people actually use/support this? | The application addresses a common need for organizing tasks and working toward goals. Its simple interface could make it useful for students, employees, and other users who need to organize responsibilities. |
| Technical — can we build it with what we have/can get? | Yes. The application can be developed as a web application using standard web technologies. The initial version can use simulated data and does not require authentication or an external database. |
| Economic — does the payoff justify the cost? | The initial development cost is low because the application can be created with available development tools and simulated data. Benefits include improved organization, easier progress tracking, and time saved managing information. |
| Schedule — can it be done in a useful timeframe? | Yes. The initial version can focus on viewing goals and tasks, completing tasks, setting due dates, and tracking progress. Additional features can be added later if time allows. |

## 5. Costs & Benefits

**Costs** (one-time + ongoing):

| Item | One-time | Ongoing/year |
|------|----------|----------------|
| Application development | $0–$500 | $0–$100 |
| Testing and maintenance | $0–$100 | $0–$100 |
| Hosting | $0 | $0–$100 |
| **Estimated Total** | **$0–$600** | **$0–$300** |

**Benefits** (tangible + intangible):

| Benefit | Tangible ($/time saved)? | Notes |
|---------|-----------------------------|-------|
| Better task organization | Time saved searching for tasks and information | Keeps goals and related tasks together. |
| Improved progress tracking | Time saved manually tracking completed tasks | Users can see completed tasks and their relationship to larger goals. |
| Reduced need for multiple tools | Potential time saved switching between applications | Combines basic goal and task management into one system. |
| Improved productivity and organization | Difficult to assign a specific dollar value | Gives users a clearer view of what they need to accomplish. |

**Payback period:** Difficult to calculate precisely because the application is primarily intended to provide organizational and productivity benefits rather than direct revenue.

**ROI:** `(benefit − cost) / cost`. A precise ROI cannot be calculated until actual development costs and measurable benefits are available.

*(See Toolkit Part C — Financial Analysis Tools document for payback, ROI, and present value formulas.)*

## 6. Priority & Urgency
The application should be developed as a focused solution for organizing goals and tasks. Without a centralized system, users may continue relying on separate tools and manually connecting their goals with daily tasks. Developing the application now also provides an opportunity to create and evaluate a working prototype while keeping the initial scope manageable.

## 7. Recommendation
Proceed with **Option B, GoalTrack**, and develop a basic web application that connects goals with tasks and allows users to track their progress.

